# Documentation

## When you need project context

If you need to understand the project (architecture, components, design tokens, Storybook, libraries, assets), read **Documentation/index.md** first and open the linked doc that matches your task. Do not guess structure or conventions—use the docs.

## When you change the project, update the docs

Whenever you:

- Add or change a **component** (atom or molecule): update [Documentation/component-structure.md](Documentation/component-structure.md) (list and one-line description). Create the file or add a subsection if it does not exist.
- Add or change **colors / design tokens** (e.g. in `tailwind.config.js`): update [Documentation/design-tokens-colors.md](Documentation/design-tokens-colors.md).
- Install or remove a **library** (dependency): update [Documentation/libraries.md](Documentation/libraries.md).
- Change **Storybook** (addons, story locations, build): update [Documentation/storybook.md](Documentation/storybook.md).
- Add or change **assets** under `src/assets/`: update [Documentation/assets.md](Documentation/assets.md).
- Change **repo layout**, **path aliases**, or **src/** structure: update [Documentation/architecture.md](Documentation/architecture.md).
- Change **setup**, **scripts**, or **troubleshooting**: update [Documentation/getting-started.md](Documentation/getting-started.md).

If the right doc file does not exist, create it and add a short entry in [Documentation/index.md](Documentation/index.md).

## How to write in the docs

- **State only**: Describe the current capabilities and configuration of the item. Do not describe specific changes from the conversation or implementation details that are not useful for future developers.
- **Concise**: Add only what other developers must know. No long explanations or chat-specific context.
- **Neutral**: Write as reference (e.g. "Button supports types: default, red, green, yellow") not as a changelog or narrative.
